%% A declaration's types are the declaring scope's.
%%
%% TGLP "Compilation", third step: every type T of every .glp file, self.glp
%% files included, is renamed to M:T, "two sibling modules have distinct
%% scopes, so two same-named types defined in them are two types, which one
%% flat namespace would otherwise make one, checking a module against a
%% definition it cannot see"; fourth step: every type reference resolves "to
%% the renamed type of the nearest scope defining it".  This self.glp's
%% `procedure tally(Stream(Answer)?, Stream(Integer))` therefore means THIS
%% Answer --- authorise ; refuse --- in every module of the directory, however
%% a module defines an Answer of its own: caller.glp does, and its own Answer
%% is what its own declarations mean.  The root self.glp's `self_key(Key)` is
%% the root's Key in the same way, whatever caller.glp calls Key.
%%
%% Until 2026-09-18 the per-module check merged the scope chain into one flat
%% name map, so a module's Answer replaced the one its ancestor's declaration
%% named: programs/social/graph/core/agent.glp:925's `authorise_link(L,
%% authorise)` was rejected against social/graph/self.glp's Answer (IGLP,
%% 2026-09-18).  Covered by glp_runtime/test/analysis/scoped_declaration_test.dart;
%% the rejected direction is ../scoped_declaration_reject/.
Answer ::= authorise ; refuse.
procedure tally(Stream(Answer)?, Stream(Integer)).
tally([], []).
tally([authorise | As], [1 | Ts?]) :- tally(As?, Ts).
tally([refuse | As], [0 | Ts?]) :- tally(As?, Ts).
imported procedure caller#go(Stream(Integer)).
exported procedure go(Stream(Integer)).
go(Ts?) :- caller # go(Ts).
